\DOC LOCAL_ADD_TWICE4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_ADD_TWICE4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Symbolically executes an inlined modular-addition subroutine over a block of
machine instructions, advancing an {eventually} goal from the current symbolic
state to a successor state and recording the resulting value together with its
congruence as new assumptions.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument is an {int} selecting which code offset/variant of the
addition block to step through ({0} and {2} occur in the observed traces). The
second is a {string list} of three names: the name to bind to the result value,
and the names of the two summand values whose stored contents are read; these
are used to form the new value and congruence assumptions. The goal's
assumptions {A} describe a symbolic machine state at step {s}.
{
   A ?- eventually ... s
   ================================================  LOCAL_ADD_TWICE4_TAC n ["t"; "x1"; "x2"]
   A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent Here {s'} is a fresh successor state. All state-describing
assumptions mentioning {s} — program-counter, loaded-code, register and memory
reads, and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — are discharged and replaced
in {A'} by the corresponding assumptions about {s'}, with the program counter
advanced and the {MAYCHANGE} relation extended by the registers, flags and
memory region written. {A'} also gains an assumption that the chosen memory
region of {s'} holds the named result {t}, and a further assumption relating
{t} to the sum of the two named operands modulo the field prime; in some
applications this congruence is unconditional, in others it is guarded by range
hypotheses on the operands. Assumptions not mentioning the state (side
conditions, non-overlapping facts, earlier value facts) are retained, and the
conclusion's {eventually} postcondition is unchanged apart from the state
argument.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-successor behaviour, always on goals
whose conclusion is an {eventually} statement about a symbolic machine state.

\ENDDOC
